><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20250708105522+10'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20250708105524+10'00'" name="380ee6cf-f4c9-4ce4-b02e-eba077495ded" page="0" coords="44.730515,264.848663,289.152100,264.848663,44.730515,255.053375,289.152100,255.053375,44.730515,254.417267,289.125946,254.417267,44.730515,244.295319,289.125946,244.621979,44.729721,243.929291,120.266449,243.929291,44.729721,234.134003,120.266449,234.134003,38.153503,233.497894,289.182526,233.497894,38.153503,223.543289,289.182526,223.702606,44.843689,223.009888,70.196182,223.009888,44.843689,213.214600,70.196182,213.214600,38.153503,212.578491,289.291870,212.578491,38.153503,202.623886,289.291870,202.783203,44.786301,202.090515,181.026749,202.090515,44.786301,192.295227,181.026749,192.295227,38.153503,191.659119,289.104126,191.659119,38.153503,181.704514,289.104126,181.863831,44.900284,181.171112,289.164825,181.171112,44.900284,171.375824,289.164825,171.375824,44.900284,170.683136,161.066147,170.683136,44.900284,160.887848,161.066147,160.887848,38.153503,160.251740,289.160919,160.251740,38.153503,150.297134,289.160919,150.456451,44.956863,149.763733,289.218231,149.763733,44.956863,139.968445,289.218231,139.968445" rect="37.847397,139.662338,289.597961,265.154785" subject="Comment on Text" title="Alexander Gibson"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-10.7pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>The frequency of bushfires in Australia is increasing and it is expected&#xD;bushfire smoke will become a more prevalent phenomenon&#xD;impacting air quality.&#xD;• Bushfire smoke can have negative impacts upon health and performance.&#xD;• Athletes exercising at high intensity for prolonged durations will increase&#xD;their exposure to air pollutants.&#xD;• Athletes, coaches and support staff should monitor air quality&#xD;(PM2.5 concentration) and make appropriate adjustments to&#xD;training duration and intensity.&#xD;• Athletes with a history of elevated airway responsiveness are likely to&#xD;be at increased risk of an adverse response to bushfire smoke exposure.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="0" rect="595.198669,13.484467,779.125549,264.829407"
/></highlight
